module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.GeneratedInDegreeOne

/-!
# Degree-one generation, read back on the homogeneous pieces

`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SheafGradedAlgebra.adjoin_grading_one_eq_top` goes
from the sheaf-level assertion that a quasi-coherent graded `𝒪_S`-algebra is generated in degree
one over an open of the base to the statement that the graded ring of sections over that open is
generated, as an algebra over its degree-zero part, by its degree-one part. **This file goes back
the other way**: from that statement about the section ring to the assertion on the homogeneous
pieces that the degree-`d + 1` sections are additively generated by the products of a degree-`d`
section with a degree-one one.

The two directions are needed by different consumers. The forward one feeds the absolute Tags
01N0 and 01N1 through the graded ring of a chart. The one proved here reads the hypothesis
"`𝒜` is generated by `𝒜_1` over `𝒜_0`" of Stacks Tags 07ZR and 0B3U -- which this repository
spells as the `Algebra.adjoin` equality, over every affine open -- back onto the pieces, where it
is what makes the canonical map `Sym(𝒜_1) ⟶ 𝒜` surjective in positive degrees.

## The argument

Everything happens inside the graded ring of sections, where the homogeneous pieces are additive
subgroups of one ring and a product of homogeneous sections is an honest ring product, so no
transport along an equality of degrees occurs.

`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SheafGradedAlgebra.prodClosure d` is the additive
subgroup generated by the products of `d` degree-one sections, with the convention that the empty
product carries a degree-zero coefficient, so that `prodClosure 0` is the degree-zero part. It is
homogeneous of degree `d`, it absorbs a degree-zero factor into its last degree-one factor, and
the family is multiplicative. Now `Algebra.adjoin_eq_span` writes the section ring as the span,
over the degree-zero part, of the multiplicative closure of the degree-one part; every element of
that closure is a product of finitely many degree-one sections, hence homogeneous of the length of
the product and a member of `prodClosure` in that degree; and the degree-`d` component of a span
element therefore only keeps the products of length `d`. So a homogeneous section of degree `d`
lies in `prodClosure d`.

## Main definitions

* `…SheafGradedAlgebra.prodClosure`: the products of `d` degree-one sections, as an additive
  subgroup of the section ring.
* `…SheafGradedAlgebra.homogeneousPart`: the degree-`e` component of an element of the section
  ring, as an additive endomorphism of it.

## Main results

* `…SheafGradedAlgebra.mem_prodClosure_of_adjoin_eq_top`: **a homogeneous section of degree `d` is
  a sum of products of `d` degree-one sections.**
* `…SheafGradedAlgebra.eq_top_of_mul_mem_of_adjoin_eq_top`: **the degree-`n + 1` sections are
  additively generated by the products of a degree-`n` section with a degree-one one**, which is
  the form the consumers use.

## Implementation notes

**The degree-one factor is written on the right.** A product of a degree-`n` and a degree-one
section is placed in the summand indexed `n + 1`, and `Nat.add n 1` is definitionally `n + 1`
where `Nat.add 1 n` is not, so the right-handed convention removes every transport from the
statements below. The same choice is recorded in the implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SymmetricAlgebraConstruction.UniversalProperty`.

**Degrees are passed as equations, not matched.**
`…SheafGradedAlgebra.mul_mem_grading'` takes `i + j = k` as an argument, and
`…SheafGradedAlgebra.mul_mem_grading` repeats the `mul_mem` field of the graded-ring instance.
Both exist for the reason the transparency note of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SectionRing` records: the index of `grading`
occurs inside the instance arguments of `DirectSum.of`, so asking the elaborator to match `1 + 0`
against `1` there, or to synthesize `SetLike.GradedMul` over the section ring, is not the cheap
`Nat` computation it looks like -- both run into heartbeat timeouts.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01NN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01NN)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07ZR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07ZR)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B3U](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B3U)

## Not done here

No tag is claimed. This is the algebraic reading of a hypothesis of Tags 07ZR and 0B3U, not a
statement of either.
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits AlgebraicGeometry TopologicalSpace Opposite
open DirectSum

namespace Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge

universe u

namespace SheafGradedAlgebra

variable {S : Scheme.{u}} (A : SheafGradedAlgebra S) (U : S.Opens)

/-- The product of a degree-`i` and a degree-`j` homogeneous section is homogeneous of degree
`i + j`. This is the `mul_mem` field of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SheafGradedAlgebra.instGradedRing`, repeated as a
lemma so that no use of it has to synthesize `SetLike.GradedMul` over the section ring: that
synthesis is what the transparency note of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SectionRing` measures as prohibitive. -/
theorem mul_mem_grading {i j : ℕ} {x y : A.sectionRing U}
    (hx : x ∈ A.grading U i) (hy : y ∈ A.grading U j) : x * y ∈ A.grading U (i + j) := by
  obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hx
  obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := hy
  exact ⟨_, (DirectSum.of_mul_of (A := A.sectionPieces U) a b).symm⟩

/-- The product of a degree-`i` and a degree-`j` homogeneous section is homogeneous of degree `k`
whenever `i + j = k`. The equation is an argument rather than left to unification: the index of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SheafGradedAlgebra.grading` occurs inside the
instance arguments of `DirectSum.of`, so matching `1 + 0` against `1` there is not the cheap
`Nat` computation it looks like. -/
theorem mul_mem_grading' {i j k : ℕ} (hk : i + j = k) {x y : A.sectionRing U}
    (hx : x ∈ A.grading U i) (hy : y ∈ A.grading U j) : x * y ∈ A.grading U k := by
  subst hk
  exact mul_mem_grading A U hx hy

/-- The unit is homogeneous of degree zero. -/
theorem one_mem_grading : (1 : A.sectionRing U) ∈ A.grading U 0 :=
  ⟨_, (DirectSum.one_def _).symm⟩

/-- The additive subgroup of the section ring spanned by the products of `d` degree-one
homogeneous sections, with a degree-zero coefficient. -/
noncomputable def prodClosure : ℕ → AddSubgroup (A.sectionRing U)
  | 0 => A.grading U 0
  | (d + 1) =>
      AddSubgroup.closure
        {z | ∃ y ∈ prodClosure d, ∃ x ∈ A.grading U 1, z = y * x}

/-- With no degree-one factors, the span is the degree-zero part of the section ring. -/
theorem prodClosure_zero : prodClosure A U 0 = A.grading U 0 := rfl

/-- The span of the products of `d + 1` degree-one sections is the additive subgroup generated by
the products `y * x` with `y` in the span for `d` and `x` of degree one. -/
theorem prodClosure_succ (d : ℕ) :
    prodClosure A U (d + 1) =
      AddSubgroup.closure
        {z | ∃ y ∈ prodClosure A U d, ∃ x ∈ A.grading U 1, z = y * x} := rfl

/-- Multiplying an element of the span for `d` on the right by a degree-one section gives an
element of the span for `d + 1`. -/
theorem mul_mem_prodClosure_succ {d : ℕ} {y x : A.sectionRing U}
    (hy : y ∈ prodClosure A U d) (hx : x ∈ A.grading U 1) :
    y * x ∈ prodClosure A U (d + 1) :=
  AddSubgroup.subset_closure ⟨y, hy, x, hx, rfl⟩

/-- The span for `d + 1` lies in a subgroup `H` as soon as every product `y * x` of an element of
the span for `d` and a degree-one section does. -/
private theorem prodClosure_succ_le {d : ℕ} {H : AddSubgroup (A.sectionRing U)}
    (h : ∀ y ∈ prodClosure A U d, ∀ x ∈ A.grading U 1, y * x ∈ H) :
    prodClosure A U (d + 1) ≤ H :=
  AddSubgroup.closure_le _ |>.2 fun _ ⟨y, hy, x, hx, e⟩ => e ▸ h y hy x hx

/-- A product of `d` degree-one sections is homogeneous of degree `d`. -/
theorem prodClosure_le_grading (d : ℕ) : prodClosure A U d ≤ A.grading U d := by
  induction d with
  | zero => exact le_rfl
  | succ d ih =>
      exact prodClosure_succ_le A U fun y hy x hx => mul_mem_grading A U (ih hy) hx

/-- Multiplying by a degree-zero section stays inside the span: the coefficient is absorbed into
the last degree-one factor. -/
theorem prodClosure_mul_grading_zero {d : ℕ} {z w : A.sectionRing U}
    (hz : z ∈ prodClosure A U d) (hw : w ∈ A.grading U 0) :
    z * w ∈ prodClosure A U d := by
  induction d generalizing z with
  | zero =>
      rw [prodClosure_zero] at hz ⊢
      exact mul_mem_grading' A U (show 0 + 0 = 0 from rfl) hz hw
  | succ d ih =>
      have hle : prodClosure A U (d + 1) ≤
          (prodClosure A U (d + 1)).comap (AddMonoidHom.mulRight w) := by
        refine prodClosure_succ_le A U fun y hy x hx => ?_
        change y * x * w ∈ prodClosure A U (d + 1)
        rw [_root_.mul_assoc]
        exact mul_mem_prodClosure_succ A U hy
          (mul_mem_grading' A U (show 1 + 0 = 1 from rfl) hx hw)
      exact hle hz

/-- The spans multiply: a product of `d₁` degree-one sections times a product of `d₂` of them is a
product of `d₁ + d₂`. -/
theorem prodClosure_mul_prodClosure {d₁ d₂ : ℕ} {z w : A.sectionRing U}
    (hz : z ∈ prodClosure A U d₁) (hw : w ∈ prodClosure A U d₂) :
    z * w ∈ prodClosure A U (d₁ + d₂) := by
  induction d₂ generalizing w with
  | zero => exact prodClosure_mul_grading_zero A U hz hw
  | succ d₂ ih =>
      have hle : prodClosure A U (d₂ + 1) ≤
          (prodClosure A U (d₁ + (d₂ + 1))).comap (AddMonoidHom.mulLeft z) := by
        refine prodClosure_succ_le A U fun y hy x hx => ?_
        change z * (y * x) ∈ prodClosure A U (d₁ + (d₂ + 1))
        rw [← _root_.mul_assoc]
        exact mul_mem_prodClosure_succ A U (ih hy) hx
      exact hle hw

/-- Every element of the multiplicative closure of the degree-one sections is a product of
finitely many of them. -/
theorem exists_mem_prodClosure_of_mem_closure {m : A.sectionRing U}
    (hm : m ∈ Submonoid.closure (A.grading U 1 : Set (A.sectionRing U))) :
    ∃ d : ℕ, m ∈ prodClosure A U d := by
  have h1 : (1 : A.sectionRing U) ∈ prodClosure A U 0 := one_mem_grading A U
  induction hm using Submonoid.closure_induction with
  | one => exact ⟨0, h1⟩
  | mem x hx =>
      refine ⟨1, ?_⟩
      simpa using mul_mem_prodClosure_succ A U h1 hx
  | mul a b _ _ ha hb =>
      obtain ⟨d₁, ha⟩ := ha
      obtain ⟨d₂, hb⟩ := hb
      exact ⟨d₁ + d₂, prodClosure_mul_prodClosure A U ha hb⟩

/-! ### The homogeneous components -/

/-- **The degree-`e` homogeneous component of a section**, as an additive endomorphism of the
section ring: keep the degree-`e` summand and throw the rest away. -/
noncomputable def homogeneousPart (e : ℕ) : A.sectionRing U →+ A.sectionRing U :=
  DirectSum.toAddMonoid fun n => if n = e then DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) n else 0

/-- The degree-`e` component of a homogeneous element of degree `n` is the element itself if
`n = e`, and zero otherwise. -/
theorem homogeneousPart_of (e n : ℕ) (a : A.sectionPieces U n) :
    homogeneousPart A U e (DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) n a) =
      if n = e then DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) n a else 0 := by
  rw [homogeneousPart, DirectSum.toAddMonoid_of]
  split <;> simp

/-- Taking a homogeneous component commutes with multiplication by a degree-zero section. -/
theorem homogeneousPart_mul_grading_zero (e : ℕ) {w : A.sectionRing U}
    (hw : w ∈ A.grading U 0) (z : A.sectionRing U) :
    homogeneousPart A U e (z * w) = homogeneousPart A U e z * w := by
  obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := hw
  induction z using DirectSum.induction_on with
  | zero => simp
  | of n a =>
      have hmul : DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) n a *
          DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) 0 c =
          DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) n (GradedMonoid.GMul.mul a c) :=
        DirectSum.of_mul_of a c
      rw [hmul, homogeneousPart_of, homogeneousPart_of]
      split
      · exact hmul.symm
      · rw [zero_mul]
  | add x y hx hy => rw [add_mul, map_add, map_add, hx, hy, add_mul]

/-! ### Degree-one generation, read back on the homogeneous pieces -/

/-- **A homogeneous section of degree `d` is a sum of products of `d` degree-one sections**, as
soon as the ring of sections over the open is generated by its degree-one part over its
degree-zero part.

This is the converse of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SheafGradedAlgebra.adjoin_grading_one_eq_top`, and it
is what reads the hypothesis "`𝒜` is generated by `𝒜_1` over `𝒜_0`" of Stacks Tags 07ZR and 0B3U
back onto the homogeneous pieces.

The argument is the span description of a subalgebra, `Algebra.adjoin_eq_span`: an element of the
section ring is a degree-zero combination of products of degree-one sections, each such product
is homogeneous of the length of the product, and the degree-`d` component therefore only keeps
the products of length `d`, whose degree-zero coefficients are absorbed into their last
factor. -/
theorem mem_prodClosure_of_adjoin_eq_top
    (h : Algebra.adjoin (A.grading U 0) (A.grading U 1 : Set (A.sectionRing U)) = ⊤)
    (d : ℕ) (a : A.sectionPieces U d) :
    DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) d a ∈ prodClosure A U d := by
  have hspan : Submodule.span (A.grading U 0)
      ((Submonoid.closure (A.grading U 1 : Set (A.sectionRing U)) :
        Submonoid (A.sectionRing U)) : Set (A.sectionRing U)) = ⊤ := by
    rw [← Algebra.adjoin_eq_span, h]
    rfl
  have key : ∀ z : A.sectionRing U, ∀ e : ℕ,
      homogeneousPart A U e z ∈ prodClosure A U e := by
    intro z
    have hz : z ∈ Submodule.span (A.grading U 0)
        ((Submonoid.closure (A.grading U 1 : Set (A.sectionRing U)) :
          Submonoid (A.sectionRing U)) : Set (A.sectionRing U)) := hspan ▸ Submodule.mem_top
    induction hz using Submodule.span_induction with
    | mem m hm =>
        intro e
        obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := exists_mem_prodClosure_of_mem_closure A U hm
        obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := prodClosure_le_grading A U n hn
        rw [homogeneousPart_of]
        split
        · subst ‹n = e›; exact hn
        · exact zero_mem _
    | zero => intro e; rw [map_zero]; exact zero_mem _
    | add x y _ _ hx hy =>
        intro e
        rw [map_add]
        exact add_mem (hx e) (hy e)
    | smul c x _ hx =>
        intro e
        have hc : c • x = x * (c : A.sectionRing U) := by
          rw [Algebra.smul_def, SetLike.GradeZero.algebraMap_apply, _root_.mul_comm]
        rw [hc, homogeneousPart_mul_grading_zero A U e c.2]
        exact prodClosure_mul_grading_zero A U (hx e) c.2
  have := key (DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) d a) d
  rwa [homogeneousPart_of, ite_eq_left rfl] at this

/-- **The degree-`n + 1` sections are additively generated by the products of a degree-`n` section
with a degree-one one**, as soon as the ring of sections over the open is generated by its
degree-one part over its degree-zero part.

This is the form in which "`𝒜` is generated by `𝒜_1` over `𝒜_0`" is used on the homogeneous
pieces, and it is the converse of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge.SheafGradedAlgebra.adjoin_grading_one_eq_top` up to
the side on which the degree-one factor is written. The degree-one factor is on the *right* so
that the product lands in `Γ(𝒜_{(n : ℤ) + 1}, U)`, which is `A.sectionPieces U (n + 1)` on the
nose; written on the left it would land in degree `1 + (n : ℤ)` and need a transport. -/
theorem eq_top_of_mul_mem_of_adjoin_eq_top
    (h : Algebra.adjoin (A.grading U 0) (A.grading U 1 : Set (A.sectionRing U)) = ⊤)
    (n : ℕ) (P : AddSubgroup (A.sectionPieces U (n + 1)))
    (hP : ∀ (y : A.sectionPieces U n) (x : A.sectionPieces U 1),
      (A.mul (n : ℤ) 1).app U y x ∈ P) :
    P = ⊤ := by
  refine top_unique fun a _ => ?_
  have hle : prodClosure A U (n + 1) ≤
      AddSubgroup.map (DirectSum.of (A.sectionPieces U) (n + 1)) P := by
    refine prodClosure_succ_le A U fun y hy x hx => ?_
    obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := prodClosure_le_grading A U n hy
    obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := hx
    exact ⟨(A.mul (n : ℤ) 1).app U b c, hP b c, (DirectSum.of_mul_of b c).symm⟩
  obtain ⟨p, hp, hpa⟩ := hle (mem_prodClosure_of_adjoin_eq_top A U h (n + 1) a)
  exact DirectSum.of_injective (n + 1) hpa ▸ hp

end SheafGradedAlgebra

end Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge

end
